      "id": 1,
      "text": "Payette began as a short‑lived camp called Boomerang, built by the Oregon Short Line between 1882 and 1884.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Payette%2C_Idaho",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1891 the settlement moved upstream, incorporated as Payette, and honored French‑Canadian fur trapper François Payette.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Payette%2C_Idaho",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Payette County Historical Society Museum offers a glimpse into the town’s early railroad and farming roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.gemstatepdr.com/visit-payette/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Payette once boasted a canning factory shipping seventy‑five carloads of peas and fruits yearly, plus sawmills and a vinegar plant.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.payettecounty.info/articles/hawley.html",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Payette became the county seat when Payette County was carved out of Canyon County in 1917.",
